ese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Liang Chang-ch\u00fc\">Liang Chang-ch\u00fc</a></div><div class=\"wst-header-forward-arrow\">\u2192</div></div></div><div class=\"wst-header-notes header&#95;notes searchaux wst-unknown-notes\"><style data-mw-deduplicate=\"TemplateStyles:r14791120\">.mw-parser-output .plainSister{display:block;font-size:93%;line-height:normal;list-style-type:none;list-style-image:none;list-style-position:outside;border:1px solid var(--border-color-interactive--hover,#AAA);float:right;clear:right;margin:0.5ex 0 0.5ex 0.5ex;padding:0;background:var(--background-color-base,#FFFFFF);color:var(--color-base,#202122)}.mw-parser-output .sisicon,.mw-parser-output .dabicon{padding-right:1ex}</style><ul class=\"plainSister\"><li class=\"sisitem\"><span class=\"sisicon\"><span typeof=\"mw:File/Frameless\"><a href=\"/wiki/Special:SiteMatrix\" title=\"Special:SiteMatrix\"><img alt=\"Sister Projects\" src=\"//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Wikimedia-logo.svg/20px-Wikimedia-logo.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikisource.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"18\" height=\"18\" class=\"mw-file-element\" srcset=\"//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Wikimedia-logo.svg/40px-Wikimedia-logo.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikisource.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x\" data-file-width=\"1024\" data-file-height=\"1024\" /></a></span></span><a href=\"/wiki/Special:SiteMatrix\" title=\"Special:SiteMatrix\">sister projects</a>: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Li_Yongfang\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"w:Li Yongfang\">Wikipedia article</a>, <a href=\"https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q107367390\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"d:Q107367390\">Wikidata item</a></li></ul></div><div class=\"ws-noexport\" id=\"ws-data\" style=\"speak:none\"><span id=\"ws-article-id\">3643686</span><span id=\"ws-title\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period\">Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period</a>, Volume 1 \u2014 Li Yung-fang</span><span id=\"ws-editor\">Arthur W. Hummel</span><span id=\"ws-author\">George A. Kennedy</span></div></div>\n<p><span style=\"display:none\" id=\"dynamic&#95;layout&#95;overrider\">Layout 2</span>\n</p>\n<div class=\"prp-pages-output\" lang=\"en\">\n<p><span><span class=\"pagenum ws-pagenum\" id=\"499\" data-page-number=\"499\" data-page-name=\"Page:Eminent Chinese Of The Ch\u2019ing Period - Hummel - 1943 - Vol. 1.pdf/511\" data-page-index=\"511\" data-page-quality=\"4\" title=\"Page:Eminent&#95;Chinese&#95;Of&#95;The&#95;Ch%E2%80%99ing&#95;Period&#95;-&#95;Hummel&#95;-&#95;1943&#95;-&#95;Vol.&#95;1.pdf/511\"><span id=\"pageindex&#95;511\" class=\"pagenum-inner ws-noexport\">&#8203;</span></span></span><b>LI</b> Yung-fang \u674e\u6c38\u82b3, d. 1634, Chinese general who served under the Manchus, was a native of T'ieh-ling, Liaotung. In 1613, when the Manchu expansion began to cause friction with the Ming Court, he was a major at Fu-shun. He attempted a parley with the Manchu leader concerning Chinese support to the Yehe, a tribe with which <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Nurhaci\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Nurhaci\">Nurhaci</a></span>&#32;[<i>q.&#8239;v.</i>] was at war. Nevertheless when the latter in 1618 disclosed his seven grounds for irritation at Chinese policy and declared open war against the Ming emperor, the city of Fu-shun became the first object of his attack. Li surrendered without a struggle and, together with the entire population, was carried off to the Manchu stronghold of Hsing-ching, \neast of Mukden. There he was given a position of authority over the conquered Chinese and was provided with a Manchu wife, the daughter of Nurhaci's seventh son. Li rendered effective service to the Manchus in the campaigns of 1619 and 1621, for which he was made a brigade-general. For refusing all entreaties of <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Wang_Hua-ch%C3%AAn\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Wang Hua-ch\u00ean\">Wang Hua-ch\u00ean</a></span>&#32;[<i>q.&#8239;v.</i>] to reaffirm his allegiance to China, the Manchus granted him advance pardon equivalent to the commutation of three death sentences. Since he was the first Chinese of importance to join the Manchus, his value to the latter was great. He helped materially, for example, in conducting the peace negotiations with the Koreans in 1627 (see under <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Amin\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Amin\">Amin</a></span>). He died seven years later, soon after receiving the hereditary rank of viscount of the third class.\n</p><p>Li Yung-fang had nine sons, the most prominent of whom were Bayan (see under <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Li_Shih-yao\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Li Shih-yao\">Li Shih-yao</a></span>), inheritor of the rank of viscount, and <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Li_Shuai-t%27ai\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Li Shuai-t&#39;ai\">Li Shuai-t'ai</a></span>&#32;[<i>q.&#8239;v.</i>] who on his own merit received the hereditary rank of a first class baron. After 1642 Li Yung-fang's descendants belonged to the Chinese Plain Blue Banner.\n</p>\n<style data-mw-deduplicate=\"TemplateStyles:r15431518\">.mw-parser-output .wst-rule{background-color:currentcolor;color:inherit;width:auto;margin:2px auto 2px auto;height:1px;overflow:hidden}.mw-parser-output .wst-rule.wst-rule-left{margin-left:0}.mw-parser-output .wst-rule.wst-rule-right{margin-right:0}.mw-parser-output .wst-rule.__uirule{background-color:#a2a9b1;color:inherit;border:0;margin-top:0.2em;margin-bottom:0.2em}</style><hr class=\"wst-rule wst-rule-left\" style=\"color:inherit;width:40%;\" />\n<p>[1/237/2b; 2/78/10a; 4/5/5b; \u660e\u5b63\u5317\u7565 <i>Ming-chi pei-l\u00fceh</i> 1/1b; Hauer, <i>K'ai-kuo fang-l\u00fceh</i> 66f.]\n</p>\n<style data-mw-deduplicate=\"TemplateStyles:r15431519\">.mw-parser-output .wst-right{text-align:right}</style><div class=\"wst-right\" style=\"margin-right:2em;\">\n<p><span class=\"smallcaps\" style=\"font-variant:small-caps;\">George A. Kennedy</span>\n</p>\n</div>\n<p><br />\n&#32;\n</p>\n</div>\n</div>"},"links":[{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Amin"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Li Shih-yao"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Li Shuai-t'ai"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Li Yung"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Liang Chang-ch\u00fc"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Nurhaci"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Wang Hua-ch\u00ean"},{"ns":102,"exists":"","*":"Author:Arthur W. Hummel"},{"ns":102,"exists":"","*":"Author:George A. Kennedy"}],"sections":[],"wikitext":{"*":"{{Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period\n | volume   = 1\n | onlysection = li yung-fang\n | previous = Li Yung\n | next     = Liang Chang-ch\u00fc\n | from     = 511\n | to       = 511\n | notes    =\n | section_author = George A. Kennedy\n}}{{DEFAULTSORT:Li Yung-fang}}"}}}